=base:summary.cmark

How readers find their way across the pages of a documentation.

=base:main-content.cmark

Every [documentation page](/documentation-layout.html) comes with the list of
all the pages of the documentation and with a search box. There is nothing to
write for either of them: both are built from the pages that use the
`documentation` layout.

# The list of pages

The sidebar lists the titles of the pages, by group, in the [order of the
documentation](/documentation-ordering.html). The page being read is
highlighted. On a narrow screen the list moves above the content, folded.

# The search box

The search box of the header looks for the typed words in the documentation
pages only: their titles, their headings and their text. A result leads to the
heading under which the words were found, not merely to the page.

Press `/` to jump to the search box, use the arrow keys to pick a result and
`Enter` to open it.

# The search index

The search runs in the browser. It needs the `doc-search.js` script, which a
site started with `kitchen-sink init` has (copy it from the `website-src`
directory of Kitchen-Sink otherwise), and it reads `/json/doc-search.json`, a
file produced for any site that has documentation pages. That file has one
entry for the introduction of each page and one entry per heading, each with
its `url`, `title`, `group`, `heading` and `text`.
